--!native
--!optimize 2
-- Texture codec ("RBA4"): short sound effects rebuilt from three kinds of generic, pre-uploaded sound pieces, each started
-- at an exact time:
--   bubbles  short tones that glide (water drops, rings, chirps): one of 120 generic tone shapes, at a speed (sets pitch
--            and length together) and a volume
--   clicks   sharp decaying octave-noise bursts (5 octaves x 3 decays x 3 variants)
--   grains   sine-windowed noise puffs on a fixed grid per band, one volume each: a half-octave set (15 bands, 44 Hz ..
--            5.7 kHz) and a sixth-octave set (34 bands, 40 Hz .. 2 kHz) that gives the lows their exact colour
-- plus a low-pass at the top of the original's own spectrum.
--
--   local bytes, stats = Texture.encode(signal, { Yield = task.wait, Progress = function(stage, frac) end })
--   Texture.isClip(bytes) / Texture.unpack(bytes) -> clip    Texture.LAYOUT (which upload file holds each piece, and where)
-- The generic upload files are tex_tones, tex_clicks, tex_grains2 and tex_loop; a kind in Texture.SplitSeconds is split into
-- tex_<kind>_NN files under that many seconds. Their ids are in Codec.Manifests.Files; Codec.Manifests puts every file of
-- Texture.LAYOUT.allFiles in its Core set (preloaded like the other generic files).
--
-- The analysis splits the sound into its steady tones and its noise (median filters
-- over the spectrogram), finds tone ridges and fits a tone shape to each (spectrogram matching), keeps a tone only if it
-- brings the rebuilt sound closer to the original where it plays AND explains far more than chance (phase-scrambled
-- copies), finds clicks in the noise (0.5 ms energy peaks per octave), and sets the grain volumes from what remains.

local AudioLab = script:FindFirstAncestor("AudioLab")
local FFT = require(AudioLab.DSP.FFT)

local Texture = {}
Texture.Magic = "RBA4"
Texture.Version = 2 -- (v2: two grain sets, tex_grains2)

